## 1. Create the container

Download the Ubuntu template on the Proxmox host:

```bash
pveam update
pveam available --section system | grep ubuntu
pveam download local <ubuntu-standard-template-name>
```

Proxmox web UI > **Create CT**:

| Tab | Setting |
| --- | --- |
| General | CT ID 230, hostname ln-glpi01, root password, Unprivileged container ticked |
| Template | Ubuntu standard template |
| Disks | zfs-nvmeT500-vm, 20 GB |
| CPU | 2 cores |
| Memory | 2048 MB, swap 512 MB |
| Network | vmbr0, static 192.168.7.83/24, gateway 192.168.7.1 |
| DNS | Domain corp.silverbank.internal, servers 192.168.7.80 192.168.7.82 |

Options > Start/Shutdown order: 5 (after the Windows lab).

**Why a container and not a VM:** GLPI is a small Linux web app. A container shares the Proxmox kernel, starts in seconds and uses only the RAM it needs.

## 2. Check the network and update

```bash
ip a                                  # 192.168.7.83 on eth0
ping -c 3 192.168.7.1                 # gateway
ping -c 3 google.com                  # internet + DNS forwarder on the DC
getent hosts corp.silverbank.internal # finds both domain controllers
timedatectl set-timezone Europe/London

apt update
apt full-upgrade -y
apt autoremove -y
```

`/etc/resolv.conf` is managed by Proxmox (lines between `BEGIN PVE` and `END PVE`): change DNS in the container's DNS tab, not in the file.

The container uses the Proxmox host's kernel, so kernel updates happen on the host, and the container rarely needs a reboot after updates.

Snapshot: `clean-updated`.

## 3. Install Apache, MariaDB and PHP

```bash
apt install -y apache2 mariadb-server libapache2-mod-php php php-mysql php-curl php-gd \
  php-intl php-ldap php-xml php-mbstring php-zip php-bz2 php-bcmath wget tar
php -v            # check against GLPI's supported PHP versions
mariadb --version
```

`php-ldap` is needed later to connect GLPI to Active Directory.

## 4. Create the database

```bash
mysql_secure_installation
mysql -u root
```

```sql
CREATE DATABASE glpi CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci;
CREATE USER 'glpi'@'localhost' IDENTIFIED BY '<db-password>';
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON glpi.* TO 'glpi'@'localhost';
FLUSH PRIVILEGES;
EXIT;
```

Test the login before using it anywhere:

```bash
mysql -u glpi -p'<db-password>' -e "SELECT 'login OK';" glpi
```

## 5. Download GLPI

Latest release from the GLPI GitHub releases page:

```bash
cd /tmp
wget https://github.com/glpi-project/glpi/releases/download/12.0.1/glpi-12.0.1.tgz
tar -xzf glpi-12.0.1.tgz -C /var/www/
chown -R www-data:www-data /var/www/glpi
ls -ld /var/www/glpi       # owner www-data
```

## 6. Configure Apache and PHP

Only GLPI's `public` folder is exposed to the web.

`/etc/apache2/sites-available/glpi.conf`:

```apache
<VirtualHost *:80>
    ServerName helpdesk.corp.silverbank.internal
    DocumentRoot /var/www/glpi/public

    <Directory /var/www/glpi/public>
        Require all granted
        RewriteEngine On
        RewriteCond %{HTTP:Authorization} ^(.+)$
        RewriteRule .* - [E=HTTP_AUTHORIZATION:%{HTTP:Authorization}]
        RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-f
        RewriteRule ^(.*)$ index.php [QSA,L]
    </Directory>
</VirtualHost>
```

```bash
a2enmod rewrite
a2dissite 000-default
a2ensite glpi
apache2ctl configtest      # must say "Syntax OK"
```

PHP session security, in `/etc/php/8.5/apache2/php.ini` (the **apache2** file, not the **cli** one):

```ini
session.cookie_httponly = On
session.cookie_samesite = Lax
```

`session.cookie_secure` stays off until GLPI runs on HTTPS (turning it on over HTTP breaks the login).

```bash
systemctl restart apache2
systemctl status apache2 --no-pager
```

Check what the running Apache PHP really sees (then delete the test file):

```bash
echo '<?php echo "httponly=" . ini_get("session.cookie_httponly") . " samesite=" . ini_get("session.cookie_samesite");' > /var/www/glpi/public/sesscheck.php
# open http://192.168.7.83/sesscheck.php  ->  httponly=1 samesite=Lax
rm /var/www/glpi/public/sesscheck.php
```

## 7. Install GLPI from the command line

The command line gives clear errors and can be reused in scripts.

```bash
runuser -u www-data -- php /var/www/glpi/bin/console database:install --help

runuser -u www-data -- php /var/www/glpi/bin/console database:install \
  --db-host=localhost --db-name=glpi --db-user=glpi --db-password='<db-password>' \
  --default-language=en_GB --no-telemetry
```

Answers: port 3306 (default), SSL **No** (the database is on the same machine), continue **Yes**. Add `--no-interaction` to skip the questions.

Expected result:

```
> Database structure created.
> Default data imported.
> Default forms created.
> Default rules initialized.
> Security keys generated.
> Configuration defaults defined.
> Installation done.
```

Log in at `http://192.168.7.83` with the default super-admin account, then secure it immediately.

(Alternative: the web installer at `http://192.168.7.83` runs a requirements check, then asks for the same database settings.)

## 8. Post-installation security

| Task | How |
| --- | --- |
| Change the `glpi` super-admin password | Administration > Users > glpi |
| Change and disable `tech`, `normal`, `post-only` | Each user > new password + untick Active > Save |
| Leave `glpi-system` | Internal account |
| Delete the web installer | `rm /var/www/glpi/install/install.php` |
| Protect the database config | `chmod 640 /var/www/glpi/config/config_db.php` |
| Application URL | Setup > General > `http://helpdesk.corp.silverbank.internal` |
| DNS record (on LN-DC01) | `Add-DnsServerResourceRecordA -ZoneName corp.silverbank.internal -Name helpdesk -IPv4Address 192.168.7.83` |
| Snapshot | CT 230 > Snapshots > `glpi-installed` |

GLPI shows a warning banner until every default password is changed. Every GLPI installation starts with the same default accounts, and attackers try them first.

## 9. Troubleshooting log

Problems met during this installation, and how they were solved.

| Problem | Cause | Fix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `nslookup` printed nothing | Minimal container quirk | Tested with `getent hosts` (works for every program); installed `dnsutils` |
| Installer: "Security configuration for sessions" not OK | Apache still running with the old PHP settings | Restarted Apache; verified the live values with a temporary PHP test page |
| `mysql -u glpi -p` "Access denied" with the right password | Proxmox console keyboard layout changed symbols typed at the hidden prompt | Tested non-interactively with `-p'<password>'`; typed passwords in the browser instead |
| "The connection to the SQL server could not be established" | `config_db.php` held an old password from a first install; the database password had been changed afterwards | Found repeated "Access denied" lines in `journalctl -u mariadb` (an app retrying), then compared `config_db.php` with the real password |
| Login "Incorrect username or password" after fixing the config | Unclear state after several partial attempts | Clean reinstall: removed `config_db.php` and `glpicrypt.key`, dropped and recreated the database, installed with `bin/console database:install` |

**Lessons**

- Test a login outside the application (`mysql -u user -p'...'`) to find which layer is broken.
- Changing a password in one place doesn't update the applications that use it. Check their config files.
- A burst of automatic "Access denied" lines in a log means a service is retrying with saved credentials.
- When an install has been half-done several times, a clean reinstall to a known state is faster than guessing.
- Use SSH from the Mac instead of the Proxmox console: the keyboard always matches and copy/paste works.

